{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And you shall have a peg (יָתֵד) upon your weapon (אֲזֵנֶךָ).</b> In tractate Ketuvot, chapter 1 (5a), Bar Kappara expounded: What is written ״וְיָתֵד תִּהְיֶה לְךָ עַל אֲזֵנֶךָ״? Do not read ״אֲזֵנֶךָ״ but ״אָזְנֶיךָ״ — your ears: if a person hears something unfitting, he should place his finger in his ears, for they are like pegs. And Tanna d'vei Rabbi Yishmael (there 5b): Why is the whole ear hard and its cartilage (אַלְיָה) soft? So that if a person hears something unfitting, he can fold its cartilage inside it. And the cartilage is the end of the ear; therefore it is soft, for the prudent one (הַמַּשְׂכִּיל) will make his ear hear the end of the dispute, for every dispute that is not for the sake of Heaven will not endure in the end, and remembering its end helps him to abandon the quarrel and make himself as a man who does not hear. But the words of Bar Kappara require explanation, for what did he see to take the verse entirely out of its plain meaning — it has no connection neither to what precedes nor to what follows — and what he expounded, reading ״אֲזֵנֶךָ״ as the language of ear (אֹזֶן), is something the ear cannot hear [as written]." "And it seems close to my mind that he wished to expound this verse in light of what is said ״כִּי תֵצֵא מַחֲנֶה עַל אוֹיְבֶיךָ וְנִשְׁמַרְתָּ מִכֹּל דָּבָר רָע״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Sifrei Tetzeh 119): evil speech — that is, that one must guard himself from evil tongue (לָשׁוֹן הָרָע). And in the Yalkut they bring on this the verse ״בְּשׁוֹט לָשׁוֹן תֵּחָבֵא״ (Job 5:21), and the reason for the matter the Ramban explained: lest discord multiply among them and they suffer a blow greater than from the enemies. And it was difficult for Bar Kappara that it says ״וְנִשְׁמַרְתָּ מִכֹּל דָּבָר רָע״ — evil speech — but did not explain by what means one should guard himself from it. Therefore he saw fit to say that the verse ״וְיָתֵד תִּהְיֶה לְךָ״ explains the nature of this guarding, and it is in the manner spoken of: ״בְּשׁוֹט לָשׁוֹן תֵּחָבֵא״ — that is, that he should hide and conceal himself as one who hides his face from it, and make himself as though he does not hear, and be among those who are insulted but do not insult, who hear their shame and do not reply. And by this he will silence the quarrel. As a certain sage said: no person ever insulted me except once — meaning, that he did not reply to his insulters, and through this silencing he [the insulter] did not return to expose his shame a second time. And thereby he covered his excrement (צֵאָתוֹ), that is, the disgrace of his shame (קְלוֹנוֹ), which is likened without doubt to the spattering of excrement. And this is the essence of a person's weapon (כְּלִי זַיִן), for in silence he conquers, not when he replies to him." "And according to this approach, ״אֲזֵנֶךָ״ remains in its plain sense as a weapon, and in the וָי״ו of ״וְיָתֵד״ this entire exposition is hinted, for every וָי״ו adds onto a preceding matter, to tell you that the guarding from all evil speech of the evil tongue is that he should place his finger — which resembles a peg — into his ear, and this is his weapon that saves him from the scourge of the tongue. And Scripture says that just as he has another peg in addition to an ordinary weapon with which he digs in the ground to cover his excrement, so too the וָי״ו adds an analogue — the finger resembling a peg with which he digs in the ear to make himself as though he does not hear, and with this he covers his shame and his excrement, as it is written (Proverbs 12:16): ״וְכֹסֶה קָלוֹן עָרוּם״. And the finger is an additional weapon beyond the ordinary weapons with which one fights a standard battle by hand. But in the battle that comes from the scourge of the tongue, there is nothing good but to hide with the finger, and this is the weapon against the mouth of one who speaks with an evil tongue, ״וַיּוֹרוּ הַמּוֹרִים בְּחֵץ שָׁחוּט לְשׁוֹנָם״, and there is no shield against them but silence. And in this manner there is no need to change the word ״אֲזֵנֶךָ״, for it is a weapon according to its plain sense. And this covering was juxtaposed to the verse that speaks of covering excrement, for the two are one matter."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The citation 'ה)' after 'פרק א׳' refers to the folio 5a in tractate Ketuvot; 'שם ה:)' later in the comment refers to 5b.", 2 "The phrase וַיּוֹרוּ הַמּוֹרִים בְּחֵץ שָׁחוּט לְשׁוֹנָם (from Psalm 64:4) is left untranslated in the body as it is a direct Hebrew script quotation; it means roughly 'and the counsellors shoot with a whetted arrow — their tongue.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:43.713426440Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}